  "full_response": "The key principles of object-oriented programming (OOP) are encapsulation, inheritance, polymorphism, and abstraction. Let's break down each principle and provide examples in Python:\n\n1. **Encapsulation**:\n   Encapsulation is the principle of bundling the data (attributes) and the methods (functions) that operate on the data into a single unit called an object. It also involves restricting direct access to some of the object's components, which is a means of preventing accidental interference and misuse of the data.\n\n   Example in Python:\n ```python\n   class Car:\n       def __init__(self, make, model):\n           self.__make = make       # private attribute\n           self.__ = model     # private attribute\n\n       def get_make(self):\n           return self.__make     # public method to access private attribute\n\n       def get_model(self):\n           return self.__model    # public method to access private attribute\n\n   my_car = Car(\"Toyota\", \"Corolla\")\n   print(my_car.get_make())  # Output: Toyota\n   print(my_car.get_model()) # Output: Corolla\n   ```\n\n2. **Inheritance**:\n   Inheritance is a to form new classes using classes that have already been defined. It allows code reusability and cleaner code structure by creating a hierarchical relationship between the parent (base) and child (derived) classes.\n\n   Example in Python:\n   ```python\n   class Vehicle:  # Base class\n       def __init__(self, brand):\n           self.brand = brand\n\n       def display(self):\n           print(f\"This is a {self.brand}.\")\n\n   class Car(Vehicle):  # Derived class\n       def __init__(self, brand, model):\n           super().__init__(brand)\n           self.model = model\n\n       def display_details(self):\n           print(f\"This is a {self.brand} {self.model}.\")\n\n   my_car = Car(\"Toyota\", \"Corolla\")\n   my_car.display()\n   my_car.display_details()\n   # Output:\n   # This is Toyota.\n   # This is a Toyota Corolla.\n   ```\n\n3. **Polymorphism**:\n   Polymorphism allows methods to do different things based on the object it is acting upon. In OOP, polymorphism usually means that a parent class reference can point to a child class object.\n\n   Example in Python:\n   ```python\n   class Shape:\n def draw(self):\n           raise NotImplementedError(\"Subclasses must implement this!\")\n\n   class Circle(Shape",
